1si|n|To support, either by furnishing strength or means in cooperation to effect a purpose, or to prevent or to remove evil; to help; to assist. ; Help; succor; assistance; relief. ; The person or thing that promotes or helps in something done; a helper; an assistant. ; A subsidy granted to the king by Parliament; also, an exchequer loan. ; A pecuniary tribute paid by a vassal to his lord on special occasions. ; An aid-de-camp, so called by abbreviation; as, a general's aid.	a:rah,53,4;1gnj,9,2~rah,53,4;1gnj,9,2	v:x2k,49,2~x2k,49,2	n:x2k,49,2;1xja,29,4;1yhr,24,4;290l,0,4~290l,0,4;1yhr,24,4;1xja,29,4;x2k,49,2	N:1drv,65,4;1drt,32,5~1drt,32,5;1drv,65,4
